JAVA PRIMITIVE DATA TYPE CONVERSION

1. Incompatible Type Conversions:
The following conversions are not directly compatible and require explicit casting:
long -> byte
double -> int
float -> int
double -> byte
float -> byte
double -> short
float -> short
double -> char
float -> long
double -> long

2. Compatible Primitive Numeric Conversions:
Primitive numeric types can be converted automatically when converting from a smaller/narrower data type to a larger/wider data type.

BYTE can be converted to:
short — Automatic
int — Automatic
long — Automatic
float — Automatic
double — Automatic
char — Casting

SHORT can be converted to:
int — Automatic
long — Automatic
float — Automatic
double — Automatic
byte — Casting
char — Casting

CHAR can be converted to:
int — Automatic
long — Automatic
float — Automatic
double — Automatic
byte — Casting
short — Casting

INT can be converted to:
long — Automatic
float — Automatic
double — Automatic
byte — Casting
short — Casting
char — Casting

LONG can be converted to:
float — Automatic
double — Automatic
byte — Casting
short — Casting
char — Casting
int — Casting

FLOAT can be converted to:
double — Automatic
byte — Casting
short — Casting
char — Casting
int — Casting
long — Casting

DOUBLE can be converted to:
byte — Casting
short — Casting
char — Casting
int — Casting
long — Casting
float — Casting

3. Wrapper Classes and Primitive Data Types
The following wrapper classes correspond directly to their respective primitive data types:
boolean - Boolean
byte - Byte
short - Short
char - Character
int - Integer
long - Long
float - Float
double - Double

NOTE:
These conversions between primitive types and their corresponding wrapper classes are handled through boxing and unboxing.
